WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.120
America can’t afford a used car anymore… And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.120 --> 00:00:11.320
it sure feels that way. The average&nbsp;
used car listing is $27,239.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.320 --> 00:00:17.000
That’s very close to the peak price in the&nbsp;
pandemic. Why are we back up at pandemic levels?

00:00:17.000 --> 00:00:23.480
But, it’s not just that prices are going up.&nbsp;
Affordable used cars are disappearing. In 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.480 --> 00:00:28.160
more than half of used cars&nbsp;
sold for under $20,000. Today&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:32.400
it's under a third. Major used&nbsp;
car dealerships are collapsing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:37.800
Car repossessions, are the highest they’ve&nbsp;
been since 2009, after the financial crash.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.760
So, why are prices higher? Where&nbsp;
did all the cheap cars go? And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.760 --> 00:00:47.760
why are the oldest, and cheapest cars…&nbsp;
the ones rising in price the fastest?

00:00:54.640 --> 00:01:00.960
The data is pretty clear, the used car&nbsp;
experience, is getting more difficult. In 2019,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:05.960
$10-15K got you a 4-5-year-old&nbsp;
car with under 60,000 miles.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.960 --> 00:01:10.920
Today the same money gets an&nbsp;
8.7-year-old car with 98,222 miles.

00:01:10.920 --> 00:01:13.840
But the pandemic is years behind us now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:18.280
why are things still so unaffordable?&nbsp;
It’s not just inflation either,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:23.200
there’s a bunch of pieces which have changed&nbsp;
which mean we pay more, for older cars.

00:01:23.200 --> 00:01:27.680
To unpack all this, we need to&nbsp;
understand the system of used cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:30.640
which is like the marble, in a game of Mouse Trap.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:40.760
“A new vehicle sale is the marble at the top of&nbsp;
the mousetrap game. And when you drop that marble,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.760 --> 00:01:44.680
it’s going to go through all the chutes and&nbsp;
ladders all the way down to the bottom.”

00:01:44.680 --> 00:01:50.240
Every used car, was at one point, a new car. It&nbsp;
will gradually make its way down to the bottom.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:56.320
Dropping in price with age, mileage, and the&nbsp;
number of owners. Not every step is the same,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.640
and some are more important than others.

00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:02.480
But, this whole board was shaken up&nbsp;
during the pandemic. Specifically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:08.480
some very important pieces were broken, or&nbsp;
removed. One affecting another, affecting another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:13.640
which led that marble to where we are now.&nbsp;
Leading to America being priced out of used cars.

00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:18.920
The first of which, most people don’t notice,&nbsp;
but quietly disrupted the entire game.

00:02:18.920 --> 00:02:23.040
You might assume most used cars are&nbsp;
those bought, and then traded in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.040 --> 00:02:27.920
but a large chunk of them, especially the&nbsp;
“newest” used cars, come from leasing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.920 --> 00:02:33.480
And this is a hugely important part of the&nbsp;
“life cycle” for lack of a better word.

00:02:33.480 --> 00:02:36.040
A lease, is essentially a 3-year rental,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.040 --> 00:02:41.760
from the automaker themselves. Toyota,&nbsp;
Ford, GM, etc. Instead of buying a car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.800
the finance arm of the automaker would lease it to&nbsp;
you for about 3 years. You pay for the value the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:52.040
car loses over three years, plus interest.&nbsp;
And then at the end, you can buy it out,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:57.600
or it goes back to the automaker, who can sell&nbsp;
a clean, low-mileage, three-year-old used car.

00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:02.040
People lease, because the monthly payments&nbsp;
are noticeably lower than a loan payment.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:05.840
And automakers offered it because,&nbsp;
even if it was low monthly payments,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.840 --> 00:03:08.280
it still moved more new cars off lots,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:13.520
and every lease ending three years later brought&nbsp;
the driver back to the dealer for another new car.

00:03:13.520 --> 00:03:17.560
This was the backbone of the used&nbsp;
market, because it’s how a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:22.440
still reasonably new cars, about 3 years&nbsp;
old, moved into the secondhand market.

00:03:22.440 --> 00:03:28.720
But the pandemic changed things. Before the&nbsp;
pandemic, about 30% of new cars were leased.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.720 --> 00:03:33.560
In 2022 it fell to 18%. Might not sound like much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:40.760
just 30 down to 18… but it is. It’s a 40%&nbsp;
drop, and four in ten leases were gone.

00:03:40.760 --> 00:03:46.800
This meant the cars coming back off lease each&nbsp;
year fell from about 5.2 million to 2.4 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:53.000
Suddenly there were a lot fewer cars rotating in&nbsp;
and out of dealerships. More people buying cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.000 --> 00:03:56.880
holding onto them for longer terms,&nbsp;
means overall fewer cars on the market.

00:03:56.880 --> 00:04:01.480
Between 2020 and 2023, we saw&nbsp;
the global semiconductor crisis.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:06.920
The pandemic threw production and supply chains&nbsp;
into chaos, and everyone was fighting for chips.

00:04:06.920 --> 00:04:09.560
So… what does this have to do with cars?

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:14.440
New cars, becoming more and more computerized,&nbsp;
are all tied to semiconductors, which have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:21.280
experiencing all kinds of constraints, notably&nbsp;
in 2023. And today, it’s continuing thanks to AI.

00:04:21.280 --> 00:04:27.680
You might think “okay, a car has a few chips&nbsp;
in it, how can that drive up prices?” Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.080
not quite. Modern cars don't just&nbsp;
have a couple chips. They have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.080 --> 00:04:37.440
thousands. Modern cars typically&nbsp;
have between 1,000 and 3,000 chips.

00:04:37.440 --> 00:04:42.120
And this constraint had a few important&nbsp;
consequences. With limited chips, automakers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:47.560
built their most profitable models first: like&nbsp;
trucks, and SUVs. Why waste your constrained&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:54.080
supply of chips on the models that aren’t as&nbsp;
profitable? So fewer cars, and also: less leasing.

00:04:54.080 --> 00:04:57.880
“The OEMs really leaned into&nbsp;
building more profitable vehicles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.880 --> 00:05:02.600
such as higher trim levels, trucks&nbsp;
and SUVs. Those are more expensive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:02.600 --> 00:05:07.600
and tend to be leased less frequently.&nbsp;
Leasing is really expensive for an OEM.”

00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:12.520
People tend to get more mileage out of trucks&nbsp;
and SUVs, and leases come with mileage caps,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:17.880
as well as extra charges for wear and damage.&nbsp;
So they are less likely to lease these cars.

00:05:17.880 --> 00:05:22.440
“the attitude is that demand is strong&nbsp;
enough to not sell lower margin base models,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.440 --> 00:05:25.800
and to not spend as much improving&nbsp;
the cars year-over-year.”

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:30.640
And this contributed to something else. Which&nbsp;
explains why there are fewer leases, and fewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:37.680
used cars. That helps explain why prices are even&nbsp;
higher now, despite the pandemic being years ago.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:48.680
A huge impact on leasing, is in&nbsp;
something called incentive spend,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.680 --> 00:05:51.640
and this shows how much the&nbsp;
playing field has changed.

00:05:51.640 --> 00:05:57.720
Before the pandemic, in 2019&nbsp;
specifically, incentive spend was 11.12%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.720 --> 00:06:02.320
This is the amount the automaker spent on selling&nbsp;
incentives against the total value of the car,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:07.160
they’re trying to sell. Like cashback,&nbsp;
cheap financing, subsidized leases,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.160 --> 00:06:11.840
that sort of thing. Like how a bank might give&nbsp;
you a small cashback, if you get a mortgage.

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:16.120
2019 was a great time to be buying&nbsp;
cars. But after the pandemic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.120 --> 00:06:20.920
this changed. It reached a low point&nbsp;
of just 2% during the shortage in 2022.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:27.680
But then it reached an average of about 6.5%.&nbsp;
More, than 2, but way lower than pre-pandemic.

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:37.360
So on a car retailing at $50,000, in 2019 you’d&nbsp;
get about $5,560 off. Today it’s just $3,250 off.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:43.200
This is a huge issue that I don’t think people&nbsp;
have realized. Automakers don’t have to spend&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.560
as much effort getting people to buy,&nbsp;
and they’re selling overall fewer cars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.560 --> 00:06:52.440
and more profitable cars, so they don’t&nbsp;
have as much need to lease out new cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.440 --> 00:06:56.000
so there are way fewer of these&nbsp;
cars entering the used market.

00:06:56.000 --> 00:06:59.360
“The COVID experience showed&nbsp;
both automakers and dealers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:02.800
the upsides related to higher&nbsp;
margins and reduced sales”

00:07:02.800 --> 00:07:05.400
So not only are prices still high,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:10.000
their actual price, is even higher,&nbsp;
because discounts, are lower.

00:07:10.000 --> 00:07:14.320
And this shortage rolled downhill,&nbsp;
because those three-year-old cars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:17.840
the “leased” ones, are missing.&nbsp;
A three-year-old car averaged a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.840 --> 00:07:27.320
record $32,461 in Q2 2026. The pandemic&nbsp;
peak for that age was $32,164 in Q1 2022.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.320 --> 00:07:32.000
So these three-year-old cars today, are&nbsp;
more expensive than at the pandemic peak.

00:07:32.000 --> 00:07:38.720
And this starts to make more sense, when we look&nbsp;
at how long these cycles are. In 2021 and 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:38.720 --> 00:07:42.840
it was “wave one”. Right in the&nbsp;
pandemic. There was a huge shortage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:42.840 --> 00:07:47.320
so heaps of people went to the used car&nbsp;
market, which caused prices to explode.

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:53.560
But years later, we’re now in “wave two”.&nbsp;
That drop in leases from 2021 to 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:53.560 --> 00:07:57.480
is hitting us now, 3 years&nbsp;
out. The length of a lease.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.480 --> 00:08:02.200
So those cars that would be arriving now as&nbsp;
the main supply of good used cars… aren’t!

00:08:02.200 --> 00:08:07.600
The problem isn’t that prices are rising, or&nbsp;
that discounts are dropping, it’s the supply.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.840
And this delayed effect applies&nbsp;
to more than just leasing.

00:08:10.840 --> 00:08:16.280
About 8 million vehicles that would have been made&nbsp;
for U.S. buyers during those years never were,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:19.400
largely due to production&nbsp;
shutdowns and supply shortages.

00:08:19.400 --> 00:08:26.400
16.2 million cars were sold in 2025, up from&nbsp;
the pandemic-era low of 13.8 million in 2022.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:32.800
Forecasts for 2026 are between 15.8 and&nbsp;
16.3 million. But the main thing is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.800 --> 00:08:38.360
all of this is lower than the record&nbsp;
17.55 million vehicles sold in 2016.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:42.320
The U.S. auto industry has sold&nbsp;
roughly 16 million fewer vehicles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.320 --> 00:08:47.760
than it would have if annual sales had&nbsp;
held at the 2016 record of 17.5 million.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.760 --> 00:08:53.200
That is about a year’s worth of volume&nbsp;
gone — about half of it since the pandemic.

00:08:53.200 --> 00:08:56.400
But why are older cars&nbsp;
rising in price the fastest?

00:08:56.400 --> 00:09:06.040
Cars 15+ years old rose 13.9% in a year. Cars 5&nbsp;
years old or newer rose 3%. This is extremely odd.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:06.040 --> 00:09:11.360
Demand for nine- and 10-year-old cars also&nbsp;
remains significantly above historical trends.

00:09:11.360 --> 00:09:16.040
“We do not normally see this kind of pricing&nbsp;
pressure at the lower end of the market.”

00:09:16.040 --> 00:09:20.360
And it’s all because the shortage rolls&nbsp;
downhill. People who would have bought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:25.880
nearly new now buy older, and people who&nbsp;
would have bought older now buy very old.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:30.400
There’s more to this though. People are holding&nbsp;
onto their cars longer, and the average car on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:36.920
US roads is a record 12.8 years old, so fewer&nbsp;
old cars are reaching the secondhand market.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.920 --> 00:09:41.720
With how expensive used cars are, it makes&nbsp;
sense people are waiting longer to upgrade.

00:09:41.720 --> 00:09:46.160
But, on that point, there’s something&nbsp;
I haven’t mentioned yet. All of this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.160 --> 00:09:50.560
the less supply and higher price for&nbsp;
used cars, has impacted something else.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.560 --> 00:09:56.600
Another giant part of the equation has changed&nbsp;
as well, on why cars are becoming unaffordable.

00:10:03.600 --> 00:10:06.880
With a lot of cheap cars gone,&nbsp;
people tend to borrow more,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.880 --> 00:10:11.240
for longer. So most buyers aren’t&nbsp;
actually paying the sticker price,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:16.920
they’re financing it. Which turns out, can&nbsp;
be even more. This isn’t leasing though.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.920 --> 00:10:21.920
Customers are paying off the entire value of&nbsp;
the car, over time, almost like a mortgage.

00:10:21.920 --> 00:10:27.120
And the figures today are pretty crazy.&nbsp;
According to Edmunds in October 2026,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.120 --> 00:10:29.760
there were new record highs across the board.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:37.640
The amount financed for a new vehicle is&nbsp;
$44,664, and the average term is 70 months!

00:10:37.640 --> 00:10:42.560
But more than one in four new-car&nbsp;
buyers are now financing vehicles for…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.560 --> 00:10:46.000
wait for it… 84 months, or longer!

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:51.440
And it’s not just new cars, and in some&nbsp;
respects, used cars are even worse.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.440 --> 00:10:58.240
The average used car financed is $30,703,&nbsp;
also at 70 months, except, the average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.240 --> 00:11:07.840
down payment is $3,865, with an APR, annual&nbsp;
percentage rate, of 10.6%. New cars have 7%.

00:11:07.840 --> 00:11:11.400
That means, at $582 a month, across 70 months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:17.360
a used car will need about $40,970&nbsp;
repaid, on $30,703 borrowed.

00:11:17.360 --> 00:11:20.800
But, it gets worse. Among used-vehicle buyers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:20.800 --> 00:11:26.520
6.5% took on $1,000-plus monthly&nbsp;
payments, also an all-time high.

00:11:26.520 --> 00:11:30.800
But one of the ways to mitigate this,&nbsp;
has been the trade-in. When you upgrade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:35.560
you sell your existing car to the dealership at&nbsp;
the same time. That car, even if it’s cheaper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.560 --> 00:11:40.920
still has value. But now, a trade-in is&nbsp;
becoming something else. Negative equity.

00:11:40.920 --> 00:11:45.600
"Buyers who financed at 2022's peak prices&nbsp;
are starting to come back to trade in,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.600 --> 00:11:48.440
and they're bringing thousands of&nbsp;
dollars in old debt with them."

00:11:48.440 --> 00:11:52.360
A lot of people who trade in, still have&nbsp;
a lot of debt. But, it doesn’t disappear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:57.560
when you trade in your car. If it still has debt,&nbsp;
the dealer you’re trading into has to pay it off.

00:11:57.560 --> 00:12:03.600
And remember that calculation from earlier?&nbsp;
About 40,000 repaid on 30,000 borrowed? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:03.600 --> 00:12:10.480
what if the car isn’t worth more than the loan?&nbsp;
Someone has to cover the gap. That someone is you.

00:12:10.480 --> 00:12:14.600
The old loan, gets added to the new&nbsp;
loan. Which creates a bigger problem.

00:12:14.600 --> 00:12:21.200
Prime borrowers have a 60+ day delinquency rate&nbsp;
of 0.42%. But subprime borrowers, have a rate of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:21.200 --> 00:12:29.800
6.9%. And these subprime used-car buyers face even&nbsp;
steeper interest rates, averaging 19.4% to 21.7%.

00:12:29.800 --> 00:12:36.880
So if you don’t already have good credit…&nbsp;
getting a used car is even more expensive.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.880 --> 00:12:41.080
Which means a lot of people, eventually&nbsp;
can’t pay it off or fall behind.

00:12:41.080 --> 00:12:44.600
1.73 million cars were repossessed in 2024,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.600 --> 00:12:49.240
the most since 2009. Y’know,&nbsp;
right after the financial crash.

00:12:49.240 --> 00:12:54.840
Repossessions are especially bad, because a lot&nbsp;
of people use their cars to get to and from work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:58.840
If you lose access to your car, it&nbsp;
becomes really hard to maintain a job.

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:02.400
Now, this is only 15% of total car debt,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:08.480
but it’s having a big impact. Specifically&nbsp;
on a group outside of consumers… dealerships.

00:13:08.480 --> 00:13:13.960
What if the bank won’t even lend you money for&nbsp;
a used car? Well, the dealership, in a way,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.960 --> 00:13:17.360
becomes the bank. These are subprime lenders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.360 --> 00:13:22.280
Used car dealerships that will lend to subprime&nbsp;
buyers or those with no credit history.

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:27.600
But this is a risky strategy. And&nbsp;
now, many of them are collapsing.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:33.040
Tricolor, for example, went into liquidation&nbsp;
in September 2025, owing lenders $900M.

00:13:33.720 --> 00:13:37.880
America's Car-Mart, the biggest publicly&nbsp;
listed buy-here-pay-here dealer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.880 --> 00:13:42.600
closed 60 of its 154 stores in&nbsp;
a year, and lost $139 million.

00:13:42.600 --> 00:13:48.520
In their Q4 2026 results, they even said&nbsp;
“the Company's substantial indebtedness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:48.520 --> 00:13:53.200
its liquidity position raise substantial&nbsp;
doubt about its ability to continue”

00:13:53.200 --> 00:13:58.280
And as a result, lenders, namely banks,&nbsp;
are pulling back from this entire sector.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.280 --> 00:14:03.480
Even if prices improve… used cars for&nbsp;
subprime buyers… likely won’t for a while.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.480 --> 00:14:06.680
Places to get used cars are starting to disappear.

00:14:06.680 --> 00:14:08.080
The question now is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.080 --> 00:14:13.200
is this all a temporary downstream wave&nbsp;
from the pandemic? Or a new normal?

00:14:13.200 --> 00:14:16.080
Well, it’s looking like a new normal.

00:14:16.080 --> 00:14:20.320
“I think it’s kind of the new normal&nbsp;
outside of a big economic impact.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.320 --> 00:14:24.160
Supply is not getting a lot better&nbsp;
over the next three to four years.”

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:30.160
Every used car starts as someone’s new car. But&nbsp;
automakers began to build fewer, leased fewer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.160 --> 00:14:35.840
and discounted less. The cars that should be on&nbsp;
many lots today... never made it. Never made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:35.840 --> 00:14:42.200
down the mousetrap. And as a result everyone is&nbsp;
paying more, or moving further down the board.

00:14:43.040 --> 00:14:48.000
One big car dealership though, is doing&nbsp;
quite well. Carvana. The online dealership,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:48.000 --> 00:14:53.120
with the giant car vending machine. But,&nbsp;
they handled the pandemic a bit differently.

00:14:53.120 --> 00:14:54.920
“Unbeknownst to investors,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:54.920 --> 00:14:59.080
Carvana’s sustainable growth machine was&nbsp;
built on a fraudulent pump-and-dump scheme.”

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:03.640
Now, they’re being sued, and to find out&nbsp;
what happened, watch this video next.

